    {
      "id": "mmlu_chemistry_3559",
      "category": "chemistry",
      "want": "E",
      "got": "E",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "For an adiabatic process, $\\Delta S = 0$ is only true for reversible adiabatic processes. However, this is a reversible adiabatic process, so $\\Delta S = 0$.\n1) For a diatomic gas, $C_V = \\frac{5}{2}R$ and $C_p = \\frac{7}{2}R$.\n2) The adiabatic index is $\\gamma = C_p/C_V = 7/5 = 1.4$.\n3) For an adiabatic process, $T_1 V_1^{\\gamma-1} = T_2 V_2^{\\gamma-1}$, so $V_2/V_1 = (T_1/T_2)^{1/(\\gamma-1)}$.\n4) The entropy change is $\\Delta S = n C_V \\ln(T_2/T_1) + n R \\ln(V_2/V_1)$.\n5) Substituting $V_2/V_1$: $\\Delta S = n C_V \\ln(T_2/T_1) + n R \\ln((T_1/T_2)^{1/(\\gamma-1)}) = n C_V \\ln(T_2/T_1) + n R \\frac{1}{\\gamma-1} \\ln(T_1/T_2)$.\n6) Since $\\gamma-1 = C_p/C_V - 1 = (C_p-C_V)/C_V = R/C_V$, the second term is $n C_V \\ln(T_2/T_1)$, which cancels the first term.\n7) $\\Delta S = 0$.\n8) Numerically: $\\Delta S = 3.00 \\cdot 27.5 \\cdot \\ln(250/200) + 3.00 \\cdot 8.314 \\cdot \\ln((200/250)^{1/0.4}) = 82.5 \\cdot 0.22314 - 24.942 \\cdot 0.22314 \\approx 0$.\n9) For a reversible adiabatic process, entropy is conserved.\n\nAnswer: (E)"
    },
